  "body": "The [specification for the `<base>` element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#the-base-element) seems clear on the following:\r\n* in a `head` element\r\n* A base element, if it has an href attribute, must come before any other elements in the tree that have attributes defined as taking URLs\r\n* A base element, if it has a target attribute, must come before any elements in the tree that represent hyperlinks.\r\n\r\nBrowsers I've tried will happily ignore all that as in the following example:\r\n```\r\n<body>\r\n  <a href=\"test.html\">Click Me</a>\r\n  <base href=\"https://example.org\" target=\"_blank\">\r\n</body>\r\n```\r\nSince `<base>` tags might be injected we added an option in Content Security Policy to restrict potential targets, but it seems like that wouldn't have been necessary if browsers followed the spec. I assume browser behavior is due to historical web compat problems, but if that's not fixable we should change the spec to match reality. If the web compat problems no longer exist it would be safer if browsers followed the spec.",
